.TH TUT 1 "August 11, 2026" "TUT" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
tut \- read UTF-8 text in the terminal
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B tut
.RI [ OPTION ]... " FILE"
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B tut
is a full-screen reader for local, plain UTF-8 text.
It displays the document with Unicode-aware soft wrapping and provides
line, page, document, and literal-search navigation.
.PP
The contents of the document are treated as text, not as terminal commands.
Terminal control characters are displayed as replacement characters rather
than executed.
.PP
When
.I FILE
names a path, it must be a regular file and both standard input and standard
output must be terminals.
The file is checked while it is read; if it changes during the session,
.B tut
reports an error instead of combining inconsistent contents.
.PP
When
.I FILE
is
.BR \- ,
.B tut
reads standard input completely into a private temporary snapshot before
opening the reader.
Standard output must still be a terminal.
If standard input is not a terminal, interactive commands are read from the
controlling terminal through
.BR /dev/tty .
Consequently, a producer that does not close its output will delay the start
of the interactive reader.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Display a short help message and exit.
.TP
.BR \-V ", " \-\-version
Display version, copyright, and license information and exit.
.TP
.BR \-\-log-file = FILE
Append typed session and runtime events to
.IR FILE .
The log contains aggregate event and timing data, not document contents or
the document pathname.
An existing log must be a regular file accessible only by its owner.
The log must not be a standard stream or the input document.
The pathname may instead be supplied as the following argument, as in
.BR "\-\-log-file FILE" .
.TP
.B \-\-
End option processing.
Use this before a pathname that begins with a hyphen.
.SH OPERANDS
.TP
.I FILE
The regular UTF-8 text file to read.
Specify
.B \-
to read standard input.
To read a file literally named
.BR \- ,
use a pathname such as
.BR ./\- .
Exactly one operand is required.
.SH INTERACTIVE COMMANDS
.SS Repeat counts
A count from 1 through 9999 may precede a relative navigation command.
For example,
.B 12j
moves down twelve visual lines and
.B 5n
selects the fifth following search match.
Zero may extend a count but does not begin one.
.B Backspace
edits an active count, and
.B Esc
cancels it.
Any digit that would make the count exceed 9999 is rejected.
.SS Navigation
.TP
.BR j ", " Down
Move down one visual line.
.TP
.BR k ", " Up
Move up one visual line.
.TP
.BR Space ", " PageDown ", " Ctrl-F
Move forward one page.
.TP
.BR b ", " PageUp ", " Ctrl-B
Move backward one page.
.TP
.BR Ctrl-D ", " Ctrl-U
Move forward or backward one half page.
.TP
.BR g ", " Home
Move to the start of the document.
.TP
.BR G ", " End
Move to the end of the document.
.SS Search
Searches find literal, case-sensitive, non-overlapping UTF-8 substrings.
Match navigation wraps at the ends of the document.
.TP
.B /
Begin entering a search query.
.TP
.B Enter
Apply the search query.
An empty query clears the current search.
.TP
.B Backspace
Remove the last grapheme from the search draft.
.TP
.B Up
Recall the committed query when the search draft is empty.
.TP
.B Ctrl-U
Clear the search draft.
.TP
.B Esc
Cancel search entry without replacing the committed query.
In reader mode, clear the committed search and its highlights.
.TP
.B n
Select the next match.
.TP
.B N
Select the previous match.
.SS General
.TP
.B F1
Open or close the built-in keyboard help.
.B Esc
also closes help;
.B q
closes help when it was opened from reader mode.
.TP
.B q
Quit normally from reader mode.
It also remains available while the terminal is too small to display the
reader.
.TP
.B Ctrl-C
Request interruption.
After successful session logging and terminal cleanup, exit with status 130.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B TUT_LOG_FILE
Use its nonempty value as the session log pathname when
.B \-\-log-file
was not specified.
The command-line option takes precedence.
.SH FILES
.TP
.B /dev/tty
The controlling terminal used for interactive commands when document data is
read from nonterminal standard input.
.PP
.B tut
does not read a configuration file and does not create a default log file.
.SH LIMITS
.IP \(bu 2
The input document or standard-input snapshot may contain at most 33,554,432
bytes (32 MiB) and must be valid UTF-8.
.IP \(bu 2
Search queries may contain at most 4096 UTF-8 bytes.
.IP \(bu 2
Repeat counts are limited to 9999.
.IP \(bu 2
A grapheme longer than 1024 UTF-8 bytes is safely replaced instead of being
rendered verbatim.
.IP \(bu 2
The complete reader requires a terminal of at least 16 columns by 4 rows.
Smaller terminals display resize and quit guidance.
.IP \(bu 2
Terminal geometry is limited to 524,288 cells.
The two fixed cell vectors are also subject to a 64 MiB accounting budget;
this is not a limit on total process memory or on heap-backed cell symbols.
.IP \(bu 2
Visible rendered content is limited to 32 MiB.
.IP \(bu 2
The implementation requires Unix terminal interfaces.
Automated compatibility testing currently covers Linux and macOS.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The reader or an information option completed successfully.
.TP
.B 1
An input, terminal, rendering, search, logging, allocation, or output error
occurred.
.TP
.B 2
The command line was invalid.
.TP
.B 129, 130, 131, 143
The active session received SIGHUP, SIGINT, SIGQUIT, or SIGTERM,
respectively, and cleanup completed successfully.
.SH SIGNALS
During an active session,
.B tut
handles SIGHUP, SIGINT, SIGQUIT, and SIGTERM and attempts to restore the
cursor, alternate screen, terminal mode, and installed signal handlers before
exiting.
.PP
On SIGTSTP,
.B tut
restores the terminal before stopping.
After SIGCONT it re-enters the interactive screen and redraws the document.
